            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "The issue of ENSO-related variability in CSIRO Mk3A was taken up in more detail by Shi et al. (2008). They pointed out that the low-resolution model suffers from an equatorial Pacific cold-tongue bias, with the effect that the SST anomalies associated with ENSO extend too far west into the eastern Indian Ocean. Consequently, in austral summer ENSO-related rainfall variability over Australia was also shifted westward, from north-eastern Australia towards NWA.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "north-eastern Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "paragraph": "Consequently, in austral summer ENSO-related rainfall variability over Australia was also shifted westward, from north-eastern Australia towards NWA. The cold-tongue bias is a long-standing problem in coupled AOGCMs (Latif et al., 2001), which suggests that other models will also be affected to varying degrees.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Indian Ocean Dipole",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In that month, (Fig. 21a) Mk3.6 shows a clear southward shift of equatorial rainfall south of the Asian continent, consistent with the circulation changes shown in Fig. 20f. Note that the pattern over the Indian Ocean also resembles a negative Indian Ocean Dipole event, with positive rainfall anomalies off the coast of Sumatra, and negative anomalies south and south-west of India.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Indonesian Throughflow",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "south-eastern Indian Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The ITF is recognised as an important heat source for the southeastern Indian Ocean (Hirst and Godfrey, 1993; Schneider, 1998), and it tends to weaken in response to weaker easterly winds in the equatorial western Pacific (Meyers, 1996; Alory et al., 2007).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Leeuwin Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "These negative anomalies can be explained by a strengthening of the Indonesian Throughflow and Leeuwin Current in La Ni\u00f1a years (Feng et al., 2008); these currents transport heat from the tropical Pacific warm pool, southward along the coast.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Rossby wave train",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Southern Hemisphere",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Recently, Lin and Li (2012) used reanalysis data to show that an increasing trend in atmospheric ascent induced by a warming SST trend in the tropical Atlantic may partially explain the observed rainfall trend in NWA. Their suggested mechanism involves a Rossby wave train that travels eastward, embedded in the westerly jet waveguide of the Southern Hemisphere.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indonesian region",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The wind trends show that in GHGAS, there is weaker convergence of zonal winds over the Indonesian region, consistent with a weakening Walker circulation, whereas the reverse is true for the aerosol-induced effect in panel b.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Walker circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
